    <section class="chapter">
      <span class="eyebrow">03 · The gambit</span>
      <h2>The nickel gambit</h2>
      <p>Indonesia banned raw nickel-ore exports in 2014, relaxed the ban, then closed it
      for good in 2020. Ore exports collapsed; processed nickel — ferroalloys, mattes,
      stainless — surged. That part of the story is true, and here it is in the data.</p>
      <div id="nickel" class="chart"></div>
      <p class="big-stat">But the same file records tonnes. Between {nk ? nk.base[0] : "—"}–{nk ? nk.base[nk.base.length - 1] : "—"}
      and {nk ? nk.peak[0] : "—"}–{nk ? nk.peak[nk.peak.length - 1] : "—"}, the dollars rose
      <b class="copper">{x1(nk?.value)}</b> — and the tonnage rose <b class="copper">{x1(nk?.tonnes)}</b>.</p>
      <p>The average price of a tonne of Indonesia's processed nickel
      <b>fell {nk ? Math.round((1 - nk.unitValue) * 100) : "—"}%</b>, from
      US${nk ? Math.round(nk.uvBase).toLocaleString() : "—"} to
      US${nk ? Math.round(nk.uvPeak).toLocaleString() : "—"} a tonne. That is not a price
      shock; it is a change in what is being shipped. The basket moved from
      {nk ? Math.round(nk.composition["Nickel mattes & unwrought (HS 75)"].vBase / nk.compositionBaseTotal * 100) : "—"}%
      concentrated nickel mattes to {nk ? Math.round(nk.composition["Ferro-nickel (720260)"].vPeak / nk.compositionPeakTotal * 100) : "—"}%
      ferro-nickel and {nk ? Math.round(nk.composition["Stainless steel (7218/19/20)"].vPeak / nk.compositionPeakTotal * 100) : "—"}%
      stainless steel — products that are mostly iron by weight. Downstreaming is real, and it
      moved Indonesia into a much bulkier, much cheaper-per-tonne product. Every dollar earned
      now carries {nk ? (nk.tonnes / nk.value).toFixed(1) : "—"} times the material, and therefore
      that much more furnace, freight and fuel. Chapter 10 finds the fuel.</p>
      <p class="footnote">Values in current US$; HS92 codes 2604 (ore) vs 7202, 7501–7503,
      7218–7220 (processed); quantities in metric tonnes. The code list survives a check: at HS6,
      ferro-alloys containing no nickel are {nk ? (nk.nonNickelPeakShare * 100).toFixed(2) : "—"}%
      of the basket, and narrowing it to ferro-nickel plus HS 75 alone still gives
      {x1(nk?.narrowValue)} in value. Source: BACI.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <span class="eyebrow">04 · The paradox</span>
      <h2>More value, same place — while the neighbors climbed</h2>
      <p class="big-stat">Indonesia ranks <b class="copper">#{rankNow}</b> in economic complexity
      in {latest} — versus <b class="copper">#{rankThen}</b> a decade earlier. Fifteen times
      the processed-nickel exports; a rank that barely moved.</p>
      <p>Complexity rewards the diversity and sophistication of the whole export basket.
      Downstreaming multiplied the value of one chapter, but a single-commodity climb does
      not diversify a basket — so Indonesia held its place in the mid-seventies while its
      neighbours moved up: over the same decade {peerPhrase} places. Every line here is
      computed from the same corpus.</p>
      <p class="footnote"><b>Read the rank as a band, not a seat.</b> An independent review
      of this chapter found that the index is densely packed where Indonesia sits: in {latest},
      <b>{dens?.neighbours?.length ?? "—"} economies</b> lie inside
      {dens ? dens.eciTenPlaces.toFixed(3) : "—"} of the complexity score — about
      {dens ? Math.round(dens.tenPlacesInSd * 100) : "—"}% of one standard deviation — so ten places
      of rank separate almost nothing. This series moves a mean of
      {rv ? rv.rank.meanAbsYoY.toFixed(1) : "—"} places a year with a largest single-year swing of
      {rv?.rank?.maxAbsYoY ?? "—"}. The decade difference above is real in direction and
      <b>not resolvable in size</b>. What survives is the shape: downstreaming did not
      diversify the basket, and that is true on our numbers and on Harvard's.</p>
      <p class="footnote"><b>Rank check, restated.</b> The like-for-like test is a rank
      correlation across all economies, not a comparison of one country's seat. Re-running our
      complexity ordering against the Harvard Atlas's published HS92 index gives Spearman ρ
      between {gb1 ? gb1.min.toFixed(3) : "—"} and {gb1 ? gb1.max.toFixed(3) : "—"} in every
      year from {rv?.firstYear ?? "—"} to {latest} — a check written into this repository before
      any result was read and, until the review, never run. On Indonesia's own seat the two
      pipelines differ by more: ranked over the Atlas's own country list we place Indonesia
      #{at?.latest?.onAtlasSample ?? "—"} in {latest} against the Atlas's
      #{at?.latest?.atlas ?? "—"}, and a mean of
      {at ? at.meanGapOnSameSample.toFixed(1) : "—"} places lower across the thirty years. The
      review section below has the full comparison.</p>
      <div id="trajectory" class="chart"></div>
    </section>

